{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 280,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two of the greatest mysteries in cosmology\nare the nature of dark energy and the apparent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4610,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conflict in our measurements of the expansion\nrate of the early versus the modern universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10010,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that even dark energy can’t account for."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13090,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Could both of these be explained by looking\nto a part of the universe that we’ve largely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18510,
    "dDurationMs": 1439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ignored so far?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19949,
    "dDurationMs": 4028,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Could cosmic voids be driving the universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29320,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein completed his general theory of relativity\nin 1915, and it didn’t take long for other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34620,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physicists to figure out some pretty astonishing\nstuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38530,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe the most famous is how Karl Schwarzschild\nsolved the Einstein equations to describe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42920,
    "dDurationMs": 4409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the nature of spacetime near a compact mass\nand in the process found the equation for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47329,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48329,
    "dDurationMs": 5631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not long after, Alexander Friedmann solved\nthe Einstein equations to discover the nature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53960,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of spacetime on the scale of the entire universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57210,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Along with George Lemaitre, Friedmann’s\nsolution showed that the universe must be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61770,
    "dDurationMs": 2950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a dynamic place—either expanding or contracting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64720,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The former as it turns out, as revealed soon\nafter by Edwin Hubble’s observation of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70090,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recession of the galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72470,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That expansion seemed to perfectly match the\nuniform expansion of space predicted by Friedmann"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78290,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Lemaitre’s model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79820,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At first we thought that expansion had to\nbe slowed down under the inward gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85900,
    "dDurationMs": 1570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pull of its contents."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87470,
    "dDurationMs": 6329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But fast forward to the late 90s to the final\npiece of the puzzle when observations of supernovae"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93799,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "revealed that the universe is not just expanding,\nbut that expansion is accelerating."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98799,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The discovery of dark energy was added into\nour cosmological model with a simple modification"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104299,
    "dDurationMs": 6710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the Einstein equations—this lambda factor—the\ncosmological constant—ironically the same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111009,
    "dDurationMs": 5671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anti-gravitational modification that Einstein\nhad made as an erroneous attempt to thwart"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116680,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Friedmann and Lemaitre’s prediction of a\nnon-static universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119900,
    "dDurationMs": 4929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the addition of the cosmological constant\ncompleted our modern LCDM cosmology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124829,
    "dDurationMs": 5891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The CDM stands for cold dark matter—by far\nthe largest source of inward-pulling gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130720,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the main competitor to the outward-pushing\ndark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134970,
    "dDurationMs": 3310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LCDM seems to work very well."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138280,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we use it to model the expansion of the\nuniverse and the formation of structure, our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142670,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predictions mostly match reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144530,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But they don't perfectly match."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146930,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We talked about some mild issues with Lambda CDM's \nprediction of the finer details of galaxy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152330,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153330,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s a much bigger concern—LCDM\nmay be failing to give the right expansion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159190,
    "dDurationMs": 1250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rate of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160440,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we measure that rate of expansion in\nthe modern universe we get one number, expressed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165390,
    "dDurationMs": 2330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as the Hubble constant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167720,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then we can also calculate what the current\nexpansion rate should be by looking at the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171860,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early universe through the cosmic microwave\nbackground radiation and then calculating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175730,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how the expansion rate should have changed\nbetween the early and modern universe under"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181230,
    "dDurationMs": 2229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the LCDM model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183459,
    "dDurationMs": 4301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we do that we get a pretty different\nvalue for the Hubble constant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187760,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The modern universe appears to be expanding\naround 10% faster than it should be based"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191580,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on LCDM, and that’s including the accelerating\neffect of dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195720,
    "dDurationMs": 6970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the so-called Hubble tension, and\nit is actually making some people pretty tense."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202690,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It seems like one of two things must be true:\neither the Hubble constant measurement in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208030,
    "dDurationMs": 5179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the modern universe is wrong, or the model\nused to extrapolate that constant from the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213209,
    "dDurationMs": 2131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early universe is wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215340,
    "dDurationMs": 2350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LCDM could well be wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217690,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And actually, either of these issues could\nhave stemmed from one subtle assumption made"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223530,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right back at the beginning of this story."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226980,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When Friedmann and Lemaitre solved the Einstein\nequations for the universe they had to make"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231160,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some simplifications."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233160,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One was that matter is evenly distributed\neverywhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236700,
    "dDurationMs": 6149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s largely true according to our observations—as\nlong as you look to the largest scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242849,
    "dDurationMs": 3590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as you go to smaller scales this breaks\ndown."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246439,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just as a smooth surface of the Earth gets\nbumpier as you descend from space, first with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252189,
    "dDurationMs": 4001,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hills and mountains resolving, then finer\nstructures all the way down to pebbles and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256190,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grains of dirt."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257750,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As you zoom in on the universe, the smooth\nsprinkling of galaxies reveals clumpiness—superclusters"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263730,
    "dDurationMs": 2650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of galaxies, clusters, then galaxies themselves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266380,
    "dDurationMs": 3310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LCDM doesn’t account for this lumpiness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269690,
    "dDurationMs": 6350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But according to some recent research, the\nlumpiness might have a huge effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276040,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It could explain the Hubble tension, or make\nit worse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279010,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may even explain away the existence of dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283350,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand the effect of cosmic lumpiness,\nlet’s start with the local lump."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287820,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Milky Way lives in something called the\nLaniakea supercluster."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291340,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s this rather beautiful confluence of\naround 100,000 galaxies stretching a half"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296090,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a billion light years, all moving under a\nvast, mutual gravitational influence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301580,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unlike simple galaxy clusters, superclusters are\nnot gravitationally bound."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306630,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re loose collections of galaxies and\nclusters of galaxies that will eventually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310620,
    "dDurationMs": 3139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dissolve under the accelerating expansion\nof the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313759,
    "dDurationMs": 5991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s like gravity tried its best to form\nsomething this big, but at that scale dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319750,
    "dDurationMs": 1460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy won the battle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321210,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nice try, gravity—but at least you slowed\ndown nearby galaxies a little bit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327110,
    "dDurationMs": 7270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And because the outward velocities are slower\nthan what you’d expect from pure expansion,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334380,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we measure the Hubble constant based on\nLaniakea galaxies we should get a number that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340220,
    "dDurationMs": 1310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too small."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341530,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But wait—the number we measure in the modern\nuniverse is even higher than predicted by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346440,
    "dDurationMs": 3110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LCDM—the galaxies are moving away faster."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349550,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So does that means the Hubble tension is even\nworse than it seems?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353000,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, that was the finding of Leonardo Giani and collaborators."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356450,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They figured out how much lower our local\nmeasurement of the Hubble constant should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360630,
    "dDurationMs": 2950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be based on the gravitational influence of\nLaniakea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363580,
    "dDurationMs": 4709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They found that the measurement should be\na bit more than a percent too low due to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368289,
    "dDurationMs": 1471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supercluster’s influence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369760,
    "dDurationMs": 4351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s exciting because it makes it harder\nto explain away the Hubble tension as some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374111,
    "dDurationMs": 3898,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "local effect of lumpiness, perhaps increasing\nthe chance that we’re seeing interesting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378009,
    "dDurationMs": 1661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics in the dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379670,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, Dr. Becky has a deeper dive into\nthis paper that I’ll link below."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383950,
    "dDurationMs": 3339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, let’s zoom a little further out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387289,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the Milky Way is in an overdense region\non the scale of 500 million light years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392819,
    "dDurationMs": 6591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But looking further afield, Laniakea seems\nto form a higher density bump in a much bigger"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399410,
    "dDurationMs": 1349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underdense region."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400759,
    "dDurationMs": 5991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It seems we’re in a cosmic void that forms\na rough sphere around 2 billion light years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406750,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408530,
    "dDurationMs": 6039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the Local Hole, or the Keenan-Barger-Lenox\nVoid, and the Milky Way is pretty close to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414569,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415569,
    "dDurationMs": 4621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This underdense region should have exactly\nthe opposite effect of an overdense region."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420190,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The gravitational pull for points inside such\na region should be outwards towards all that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424800,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extra mass beyond its edge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427620,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this case the velocities we measure inside\nsuch a region should be higher than expected"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433530,
    "dDurationMs": 1849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for pure expansion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435379,
    "dDurationMs": 6181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’d have the expansion velocity plus a\nbit more from the outward gravitational tug."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441560,
    "dDurationMs": 4389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The effect of that would be to make the Hubble\nconstant look bigger than it really is—perhaps"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445949,
    "dDurationMs": 5111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explaining away the Hubble tension and saving\nLCDM."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451060,
    "dDurationMs": 2819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there are indeed a couple of studies that\nclaim just this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453879,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look at this one: Shanks, Hogarth\nand Metcalf from 2019."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458539,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They combine a djustments in our distance\nmeasurements to Cepheid variables from the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463289,
    "dDurationMs": 6451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gaia satellite with measurements of outflow\nvelocities within the Local Hole to calculate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469740,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the real modern Hubble constant should\nbe around 69 rather than 73.5, which would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476780,
    "dDurationMs": 6580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "put it in fair agreement with the values based\non the cosmic microwave background and LCDM."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483360,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So does cosmic lumpiness fix or worsen the\nHubble tension?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488050,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Are our measurements skewed more by Laniakea\nor the Local Hole?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492150,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And why is this so difficult to figure out?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495330,
    "dDurationMs": 7179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, because it’s not easy to make an accurate\n3-D atlas of positions and velocities of all the matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502509,
    "dDurationMs": 1821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out to these insane distances."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504330,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember, our giant telescopes only really\nsee faint smudges of light on the night sky."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511050,
    "dDurationMs": 5239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have to infer distances from a chain of\npotentially biased steps, and we only measure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516289,
    "dDurationMs": 4651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the component of these galaxies's velocities towards\nor away from us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520940,
    "dDurationMs": 6339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The astronomers trying to do this are very,\nvery careful, but they don’t always agree with each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527279,
    "dDurationMs": 5031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, a team including one of the discoverers\nof dark energy, Adam Reiss, claims that the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532310,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Local Hole is barely even there based on the\natlas they made with supernova distance measurements."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540310,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And to be fair, it’s weird if the Local\nHole exists at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545170,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s just stupidly large at two billion\nlight years across."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549370,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some argue that the standard LCDM model shouldn’t\nallow for lumps that big—at least not starting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555410,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the miniscule lumps we see in the cosmic\nmicrowave background."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559430,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So maybe the hole is an illusion, or maybe\nits existence points to another issue with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564050,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LCDM."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565170,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that is certainly the stance of the authors\nof a paper that came out just last month,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570060,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who argue that they can explain the local\nhole with a model that uses modified Newtonian"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575940,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dynamics—a variant gravitational model that\npurports to also explain dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580620,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I should add that MOND has more and more points\nagainst it than for it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585880,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there’s another Dr. Becky link below\nfor the latest on that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589960,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, let’s zoom out one more time—now to\nthe entire universe—to look at the effect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595980,
    "dDurationMs": 1950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of lumpiness on the largest scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597930,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s at least one proposal for structure\nnot just changing the apparent effect of dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603820,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy, but for structure being the entire cause of dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609130,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea, published by a team of Iranian scientists,\nis that dark energy is just the sum total"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615050,
    "dDurationMs": 3789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effect of all cosmic voids."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618839,
    "dDurationMs": 4991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To see how THAT works let’s have a quick\nrefresher on dark energy—and we have a whole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623830,
    "dDurationMs": 3370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "playlist if you want the gory details."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627200,
    "dDurationMs": 6210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark energy is usually thought of as the energy\nof the vacuum—the faint buzz of space itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633410,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For complicated reasons, space having a non-zero energy density should cause accelerating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639180,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640180,
    "dDurationMs": 5659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our boy Alexander Friedmann’s equations,\ndark energy ends up having the same physics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645839,
    "dDurationMs": 1821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a gas with negative pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647660,
    "dDurationMs": 4679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s an inward pulling pressure, which,\ncounterintuitively, leads to accelerating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652339,
    "dDurationMs": 4901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion if you fill the universe with the\nstuff so the inward-pulling aspect cancels"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657240,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659110,
    "dDurationMs": 2849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But you know what else has negative pressure?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661959,
    "dDurationMs": 1370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bubbles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663329,
    "dDurationMs": 5611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both bubbles and droplets occur when a pocket\nof fluid is constrained by a surface that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668940,
    "dDurationMs": 1550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has a surface tension."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670490,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Surface tension results when the molecules\nof the surface are attracted to each other,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674810,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holding the bubble together and forming a\nsphere as they try to minimise surface area."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680200,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While the inside of the bubble may push outwards—so\npositive pressure—the surface resists that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686480,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "push—which results in an effective negative\npressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690860,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So maybe dark energy is due to space bubbles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694070,
    "dDurationMs": 2630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AKA cosmic voids."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696700,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the early universe matter was spread out\npretty smoothly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700770,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gravity started pulling matter into the first\nlumps that would become galaxy clusters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704820,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe was expanding rapidly at the\nsame time, throwing these dense regions apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708950,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Material that was still falling towards these\nregions pulled itself together into great"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713570,
    "dDurationMs": 3699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sheets and filaments flowing towards the clusters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717269,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The result is the cosmic web—an interlocking\nnetwork of clusters and intercluster filaments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723459,
    "dDurationMs": 6811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But all this clumping of matter left vast\nregions that are almost empty—cosmic voids."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730270,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The team behind this recent paper argue that\nyou can treat these voids as growing bubbles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736010,
    "dDurationMs": 3639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whose surfaces are made of these sheets and\nfilaments of galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739649,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those galaxies are really moving under the\ngravitational pull of each other, falling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744839,
    "dDurationMs": 4981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "towards the highest density regions, and they’re\nalso moving apart due to the expansion of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749820,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750820,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But you can also think of them as moving apart\nfrom each other due to the expansion of these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756740,
    "dDurationMs": 2039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "giant void bubbles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758779,
    "dDurationMs": 4161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They are sort of pushed apart against their mutual\ngravitational pull, which creates something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762940,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like a surface tension, and so functionally\nproduces negative pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767500,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The study in question argues that the resulting\nnegative pressure of these void bubbles is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771980,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough to explain all of dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775060,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now I haven’t found a lot of commentary\non this result and haven’t thought hard"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780100,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough about it myself to know whether there’s\nmerit here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783670,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in general it’s a good idea to wonder\nif the crude predictions of the Friedmann"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787880,
    "dDurationMs": 5519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Lemaitre cosmological model might break\ndown if you don’t assume that matter is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793399,
    "dDurationMs": 1861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perfectly smooth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795260,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Others have thought a lot about the idea of\nthis “back-reaction” of detail structure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800260,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the global effect of gravity, so it’s\nnot silly to wonder about this stuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805390,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And a fun implication if this hypothesis is right\nis that it means dark energy should change"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810300,
    "dDurationMs": 5529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over time, first increasing and then decreasing,\nas the void bubbles first form and then in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815829,
    "dDurationMs": 1721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the future dissolve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817550,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is all pretty speculative—there are\ngood reasons why dark energy might be a simple"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823660,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vacuum energy, which means it’s not caused\nby cosmic voids, and which means the Hubble"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828440,
    "dDurationMs": 5839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tension probably has to be some local bias\nin our measurement of the Hubble constant—perhaps"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834279,
    "dDurationMs": 1981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to our local cosmic void."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836260,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Either way, if you want to understand the\nuniverse on the largest scales it turns out it's important"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842190,
    "dDurationMs": 7449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to also understand the details—especially\nof the voids—the least populated and least"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849639,
    "dDurationMs": 4196,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "studied, but perhaps most consequential regions\nof spacetime."
    } ]
  } ]
}
